n: 3.2.2
name: ids
kind: hub
is: The http/ module has nine id spaces, none derived from content: seven are arrival-order counters or positions, two come from outside (a compiled language list, the grammar's symbol numbers). Label numbers differ between runs of one binary, so the name is the identity and the number a per-process handle. A node id is a byte offset plus a depth.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/nums-model

n: 3.2.2.1
name: nine-id-spaces
kind: observation
is: http/ has nine id spaces. Label, colour, folder and identifier ids are table length plus one on first use; a file id is a per-directory count, or 0 for in-memory content. A node id is {start byte, level}, unique as folder.file.start.level. Language ids are a compiled list of 13, symbol ids the tree-sitter grammar's. No id is computed from content.
status: observed
src: 2.5.5#327-342 2.5.5#12-27 2.5.9#182-198 2.5.10#31-40 2.5.10#68-69 2.5.2#23-26 2.5.2#687-704 2.5.27#29-47 2.5.11#225-231 2.5.20#900 2.5.33#13-52
limit: A file read from a directory gets the length of that listing's file table plus one (folder.go:37); a file made from in-memory content gets id 0 (folder.go:69), which is why traced node ids read 1.0.start.level. Symbol ids are read through SymbolCount and SymbolName of the tree-sitter language (language.go:225-231). A design note, id-sample.txt, sketches a target id space as nested YAML whose names carry label paths; no code reads it.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/nums-model

